// The cost regression's subject: the smallest possible program.
//
// run.sh builds this twice -- once with --no-std and once with idstd attached --
// and records the wall time and the binary size of each. There is no dead-code
// elimination yet, so every function in the library reaches the emitted C of
// this program even though it calls none of them, and the difference between the
// two builds is the tax every `id` program on this machine now pays.
//
// That number is also the one that will say whether dead-code elimination works
// once it lands: with it, these two builds should be indistinguishable.
//
// It calls nothing from idstd deliberately. A hello-world that used fx_max would
// measure something else.

main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  print("hello world");
} return int 0;
